@page "/explain"
@implements IAsyncDisposable
@inject DemoCatalogue Catalogue
@inject ExplainLab Lab
@inject IJSRuntime JS

<h1>How it works</h1>
<p class="lead">
	A fingerprint is a short list of numbers that survives noise, compression and a different start time, and still points back to one
	recording. This page builds one, step by step, from real data. Everything is live: move the sliders, click the dots.
</p>

<SeedGate>

<div class="card">
	<div class="row">
		<label>Track&nbsp;
			<select @bind="TrackId" @bind:after="RebuildAsync">
				@foreach (var track in Catalogue.Tracks.Where(t => t.Samples is not null))
				{
					<option value="@track.Id">@track.Title</option>
				}
			</select>
		</label>
		<label>Show 12 s starting at <strong>@viewStart</strong> s
			<input type="range" min="0" max="28" step="1" @bind="viewStart" @bind:event="oninput" @onchange="RebuildAsync" />
		</label>
	</div>
	<p class="note">The tracks are generated music (no recordings are involved), but the analysis is exactly what runs on real audio.</p>
</div>

@if (error is not null)
{
	<div class="card"><pre style="white-space:pre-wrap;color:var(--bad)">@error</pre></div>
}

<h2>1. Sound as numbers</h2>
<p>
	A microphone measures air pressure thousands of times a second. The library resamples everything to <strong>11,025 samples per second</strong>,
	so one second of music is a list <em>x</em><sub>0</sub>, <em>x</em><sub>1</sub>, &hellip;, <em>x</em><sub>11024</sub> of 11,025 numbers.
	By the sampling theorem (Nyquist) a rate of <em>f</em><sub>s</sub> can represent frequencies up to <em>f</em><sub>s</sub>/2, here <strong>5,512 Hz</strong>,
	which holds the notes, harmonics and consonants that identify a recording and leaves out the top octave that radio and codecs damage anyway.
</p>

<h2>2. A spectrogram: how much of each frequency, when</h2>
<p>
	A single Fourier transform of a whole song would say <em>which</em> frequencies occur but not <em>when</em>. So we cut the signal into overlapping
	windows of <em>N</em> = 1,024 samples (92.9 ms), multiply each by a Hann window <em>w</em><sub>n</sub> = &frac12;(1 &minus; cos(2&pi;<em>n</em>/<em>N</em>))
	to avoid edge effects, and take the discrete Fourier transform:
</p>
<p class="formula">
	X<sub>k</sub>(m) = &sum;<sub>n=0</sub><sup>N&minus;1</sup> w<sub>n</sub> &middot; x<sub>mH+n</sub> &middot; e<sup>&minus;2&pi;i&thinsp;kn/N</sup>,&emsp; power = |X<sub>k</sub>(m)|&sup2;
</p>
<p>
	Frame <em>m</em> starts <em>H</em> = 256 samples (23.2 ms) after frame <em>m</em>&minus;1, so neighbouring windows overlap by 75%.
	Bin <em>k</em> is the frequency <em>k</em>&middot;<em>f</em><sub>s</sub>/<em>N</em> = <em>k</em> &times; 10.77 Hz, so the frequency resolution is about 1/(window length):
	a longer window sharpens frequency and blurs time, a shorter one the reverse (the Fourier uncertainty trade-off, &Delta;<em>f</em>&middot;&Delta;<em>t</em> of order 1).
	The picture below is the power in decibels, brighter meaning louder.
</p>

<div class="row">
	<label><input type="checkbox" @bind="showSpectrogram" @bind:after="RedrawAsync" /> spectrogram</label>
	<label><input type="checkbox" @bind="showPeaks" @bind:after="RedrawAsync" /> peaks (step 3)</label>
	<label><input type="checkbox" @bind="showPairs" @bind:after="RedrawAsync" /> landmark pairs (step 4)</label>
	<span class="note">Click a blue dot to choose an anchor for step 4.</span>
</div>
<canvas id="explainCanvas" width="1000" height="440"></canvas>
<p class="note">
	Horizontal axis: time in the track (seconds). Vertical axis: frequency (kHz). Steady notes are horizontal bands with harmonics stacked above them;
	drums are vertical smears; the dark gaps are rests.
</p>

<h2>3. Keep only the peaks</h2>
<p>
	A cell is a <strong>peak</strong> if its power is the largest in a neighbourhood of &plusmn;8 bins by &plusmn;3 frames (about &plusmn;86 Hz by &plusmn;70 ms), and above a small floor.
	Why peaks? Suppose the recording is made louder, or its loudness is changed by any increasing function: if <em>a</em> &gt; <em>b</em> then <em>g</em>(<em>a</em>) &gt; <em>g</em>(<em>b</em>),
	so <strong>every local maximum stays where it is</strong>. Noise adds a little to each cell, but a peak well above the noise floor stays the largest in its neighbourhood.
	Quiet detail, which is what noise and compression destroy, is simply not used.
</p>
<p>
	Two refinements keep the fingerprint useful. <strong>Band balancing</strong>: the spectrum is split into seven octave-like bands and each band keeps only its strongest
	4 peaks per 0.74 s, so a loud bass line cannot crowd out the hi-hat. That also caps the amount of data: this view holds
	<strong>@(view?.Peaks.Count ?? 0) peaks</strong> in 12 s, about @(view is null ? 0 : (view.Peaks.Count / 12.0).ToString("0")) per second.
</p>

<h2>4. Pair peaks into landmarks</h2>
<p>
	One peak says little: a note at 440 Hz appears in a thousand songs. A <em>pair</em> of peaks says much more. Take an <strong>anchor</strong> peak at (time <em>t</em><sub>1</sub>, frequency <em>f</em><sub>1</sub>),
	look in the <strong>target zone</strong> after it (the yellow box: up to 63 frames, 1.46 s, ahead, and &plusmn;128 bins either side) and join it to up to 6 peaks
	there. Each pair becomes one number built from three small ones:
</p>
<p class="formula">hash = ( f<sub>1</sub> : 9 bits ) ( f<sub>2</sub> &minus; f<sub>1</sub> + 128 : 8 bits ) ( t<sub>2</sub> &minus; t<sub>1</sub> : 6 bits )</p>
<p>
	The <strong>landmark</strong> is the hash plus <em>t</em><sub>1</sub>. Note what the hash leaves out: the absolute time. It contains only a frequency and two
	<em>differences</em>, so it is <strong>unchanged if the whole clip is moved in time</strong>. That single property is what lets a clip that starts anywhere find its place (step 5).
	Click a blue dot above to see its zone and the hashes it makes:
</p>

@if (anchor is not null)
{
	<div class="card">
		<p>
			Anchor at <strong>@FrameSeconds(anchor.Value.Frame).ToString("0.00") s</strong>, bin @anchor.Value.Bin
			(<strong>@HzOf(anchor.Value.Bin) Hz</strong>). @anchorPairs.Count pair@(anchorPairs.Count == 1 ? "" : "s") in its zone:
		</p>
		@if (anchorPairs.Count > 0)
		{
			<table>
				<thead>
					<tr><th>Partner</th><th>f<sub>1</sub> (9 bits)</th><th>&Delta;f + 128 (8 bits)</th><th>&Delta;t (6 bits)</th><th>32-bit hash</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					@foreach (var pair in anchorPairs)
					{
						<tr>
							<td>@FrameSeconds(pair.PartnerFrame).ToString("0.00") s, @HzOf(pair.PartnerBin) Hz</td>
							<td class="mono">@Bits(pair.AnchorBin, 9) <span class="note">(@pair.AnchorBin)</span></td>
							<td class="mono">@Bits(pair.DeltaBins + 128, 8) <span class="note">(@pair.DeltaBins)</span></td>
							<td class="mono">@Bits(pair.DeltaFrames, 6) <span class="note">(@pair.DeltaFrames)</span></td>
							<td class="mono">0x@(pair.Hash.ToString("X8"))</td>
						</tr>
					}
				</tbody>
			</table>
		}
		<p class="note">
			The three fields sit side by side in the hash (the top 9 bits are spare). The &Delta;f field is stored with 128 added so that it is never negative.
			Brackets show the plain value.
		</p>
	</div>
}

<h2>5. Finding a clip: voting on the offset</h2>
<p>
	The database stores every track's landmarks as <code>hash &rarr; (track, <em>t</em><sub>1</sub>)</code>. A clip is fingerprinted the same way, and every one of its hashes is looked up.
	A hit says: &ldquo;this clip landmark at clip-time <em>t</em><sub>c</sub> looks like that track landmark at track-time <em>t</em><sub>T</sub>&rdquo;, and therefore
	<strong>if the clip really comes from this track, the clip started <em>t</em><sub>T</sub> &minus; <em>t</em><sub>c</sub> into it</strong>. Compute that <em>offset</em> for every hit
	and make a histogram:
</p>
<ul>
	<li>Genuine hits all come from the same moment, so they all give the <strong>same</strong> offset: a tall spike.</li>
	<li>Coincidental hits (a similar pair of notes elsewhere in the track, or in another track) give offsets with no pattern: a low, spread-out floor.</li>
</ul>
<p>
	So the question &ldquo;where in the track is this clip?&rdquo; becomes &ldquo;where is the spike?&rdquo;, and &ldquo;is it really this track?&rdquo; becomes &ldquo;how many times taller than the floor is the spike?&rdquo;.
	That is what the matcher scores (using a Poisson model of the floor).
</p>

<div class="card">
	<h3>Try it</h3>
	<div class="row">
		<label>Clip starts at <strong>@clipStart</strong> s
			<input type="range" min="0" max="26" step="1" @bind="clipStart" @bind:event="oninput" @onchange="RunClipAsync" />
		</label>
		<label>plus <strong>@shift</strong> samples (<strong>@((shift * 1000.0 / DemoCatalogue.SampleRate).ToString("0.0")) ms</strong>, @((shift / 256.0).ToString("0.00")) of a frame)
			<input type="range" min="0" max="255" step="1" @bind="shift" @bind:event="oninput" @onchange="RunClipAsync" />
		</label>
	</div>
	<p class="note">The clip is 6 seconds long and is cut from the chosen track, then fingerprinted on <em>its own</em> frame grid.</p>
</div>

@if (trial is not null)
{
	<div class="hist-wrap">
		<div class="hist">
			@for (var i = 0; i < trial.Histogram.Count; i++)
			{
				var votes = trial.Histogram[i];
				<div class="@(IsPeakBin(i) ? "peak" : "")" style="height:@(Math.Max(1, votes * 100 / Math.Max(1, trial.Histogram.Max())))%" title="offset @OffsetLabel(i): @votes votes"></div>
			}
		</div>
		@if (trial.Histogram.Count > 0)
		{
			<div class="marker" style="left:@(MarkerPercent().ToString("0.0", System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))%" title="true offset"></div>
		}
	</div>
	<p class="note">
		Horizontal axis: the offset (track time minus clip time), from @OffsetSeconds(trial.HistogramFirstFrame).ToString("0.0") s to
		@OffsetSeconds(trial.HistogramFirstFrame + (trial.Histogram.Count * trial.HistogramBinFrames)).ToString("0.0") s. Vertical axis: number of hits (votes).
		The white line is the true offset, @OffsetSeconds(trial.TrueOffsetFrames).ToString("0.000") s.
	</p>
	<div class="grid">
		<div class="card"><div class="stat">@trial.ClipLandmarks</div><p>landmarks in the clip</p></div>
		<div class="card"><div class="stat">@trial.PeakVotes</div><p>votes in the spike (offset @OffsetSeconds(trial.FoundOffsetFrames).ToString("0.000") s)</p></div>
		<div class="card"><div class="stat">@(trial.BackgroundVotes < 0.05 ? "very many" : (trial.PeakVotes / trial.BackgroundVotes).ToString("0") + "x")</div><p>spike over the chance level (about @trial.BackgroundVotes.ToString("0.0") votes per window)</p></div>
	</div>
}

<h2>6. The sampling-phase problem, and why it does not matter</h2>
<p>
	The track was analysed with frame boundaries at multiples of 256 samples from its start. A clip you record begins at some arbitrary sample, so its own frame grid is
	shifted by a <strong>phase</strong> &phi; = (start sample) mod 256, anywhere from 0 to 23 ms. Two things could go wrong:
</p>
<ol>
	<li>A peak that fell in frame <em>m</em> of the track might fall in frame <em>m</em>&plusmn;1 of the clip, so the time difference &Delta;<em>t</em> of a pair changes by one frame and the hash is <em>different</em>.</li>
	<li>Offsets are measured in whole frames, but the true offset (start sample / 256) usually is not a whole number.</li>
</ol>
<p>The design handles both, and you can check it with the slider above or the sweep below:</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Overlap makes the spectrum change smoothly.</strong> Windows overlap by 75%, so moving the grid by &phi; &lt; 256 samples changes each frame's spectrum only slightly: it is a continuous function of the shift. A peak that is the local maximum in the track's frame is usually still a local maximum in the clip's nearest frame, because the neighbourhood is &plusmn;3 frames wide.</li>
	<li><strong>Not every landmark has to survive.</strong> A clip has hundreds of landmarks and the vote needs only a few dozen to agree. The ones that flip a frame simply drop out of the spike (the sweep shows what share survives).</li>
	<li><strong>The vote pools neighbouring offsets.</strong> The matcher adds the votes at offsets within &plusmn;1 frame, so landmarks that disagree by one frame about the offset still land in the same window.</li>
	<li><strong>The cost is one frame of precision.</strong> The reported position is therefore correct to about a frame (23 ms): the fractional part &phi;/256 is not recovered.</li>
</ul>

<div class="row">
	<button @onclick="SweepAsync" disabled="@sweeping">Sweep eight phases (0 to 224 samples)</button>
	<span class="note">Same clip, shifted a little each time. Watch the spike stay where it should be.</span>
</div>

@if (sweep.Count > 0)
{
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Shift (samples)</th><th>Grid misalignment</th><th>Clip landmarks</th>
				<th>At the true offset &plusmn;1 frame</th><th>Share</th><th>Spike votes</th><th>Spike at (s)</th><th>True offset (s)</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			@foreach (var row in sweep)
			{
				<tr>
					<td>@row.ShiftSamples</td>
					<td>@Misalignment(row).ToString("0.00") of a frame</td>
					<td>@row.ClipLandmarks</td>
					<td>@row.AtTrueOffset</td>
					<td>@((100.0 * row.AtTrueOffset / Math.Max(1, row.ClipLandmarks)).ToString("0"))%</td>
					<td>@row.PeakVotes</td>
					<td>@OffsetSeconds(row.FoundOffsetFrames).ToString("0.000")</td>
					<td>@OffsetSeconds(row.TrueOffsetFrames).ToString("0.000")</td>
				</tr>
			}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p class="note">
		<strong>Grid misalignment</strong> is how far the clip's frame boundaries sit from the track's, as a fraction of a frame (0 = lined up, 0.5 = as far out as possible).
		The share of the clip's landmarks found at the true offset runs from <strong>@SweepShare(min: true)%</strong> (worst alignment) to <strong>@SweepShare(min: false)%</strong> (best):
		that is the real cost of the phase problem, and it is why a clip needs a few hundred landmarks and not a handful. But the spike always sits within about one frame (0.023 s)
		of the true offset and stays far above the chance level, so the clip is still found, at every phase.
	</p>
}

<h2>7. How sure is it?</h2>
<p>
	If hits fell at random, the number in any one offset window would follow a Poisson distribution with a small mean &lambda; (the chance level above). The probability of seeing
	<em>k</em> or more in one window is <em>P</em> = &sum;<sub><em>j</em>&ge;<em>k</em></sub> e<sup>&minus;&lambda;</sup>&lambda;<sup><em>j</em></sup>/<em>j</em>!, and the library reports the score
	&minus;log<sub>10</sub> <em>P</em>. A score of 20 means &ldquo;one chance in 10<sup>20</sup> by coincidence&rdquo;. Real music is more repetitive than the model assumes, so the score is
	calibrated against measured outcomes to give a confidence between 0 and 1.
</p>

</SeedGate>

@code {
	private const int ViewSeconds = 12;
	private const int ClipSeconds = 6;

	private IJSObjectReference? module;
	private DotNetObjectReference<Explain>? self;
	private string? trackId;
	private string? error;
	private int viewStart;
	private bool showSpectrogram = true;
	private bool showPeaks = true;
	private bool showPairs = true;
	private ExplainView? view;
	private SpectralPeak? anchor;
	private List<PairView> anchorPairs = [];
	private int clipStart = 8;
	private int shift;
	private ClipTrial? trial;
	private List<ClipTrial> sweep = [];
	private bool sweeping;
	private bool built;
	private bool imageDirty = true;

	/// <summary>When set in the address (explain?auto=true), runs the demonstrations on load, so the page can be linked to or captured.</summary>
	[SupplyParameterFromQuery]
	public bool Auto { get; set; }

	private string? TrackId
	{
		get => trackId ?? Catalogue.Tracks.FirstOrDefault(t => t.Samples is not null)?.Id;
		set => trackId = value;
	}

	private CatalogueTrack? CurrentTrack => Catalogue.Find(TrackId ?? string.Empty);

	protected override void OnInitialized() => Catalogue.Changed += OnCatalogueChanged;

	private void OnCatalogueChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

	protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
	{
		if (built || !Catalogue.Seeded)
		{
			return;
		}

		built = true;
		try
		{
			self = DotNetObjectReference.Create(this);
			await RebuildAsync();
			await (await ModuleAsync()).InvokeVoidAsync("enablePeakPicking", "explainCanvas", self);
			await RunClipAsync();
			if (Auto)
			{
				await SweepAsync();
			}
		}
		catch (Exception ex)
		{
			error = ex.ToString();
		}

		StateHasChanged();
	}

	private async Task<IJSObjectReference> ModuleAsync() => module ??= await JS.InvokeAsync<IJSObjectReference>("import", "./js/audio.js");

	private async Task RebuildAsync()
	{
		if (CurrentTrack is not { } track)
		{
			return;
		}

		view = Lab.BuildView(track, viewStart, ViewSeconds);
		imageDirty = true;
		anchor = null;
		anchorPairs = [];

		// Start with an anchor that has several partners, so the zone and the table have something to show.
		var busiest = view.Pairs.GroupBy(p => (p.AnchorFrame, p.AnchorBin)).OrderByDescending(g => g.Count()).FirstOrDefault();
		if (busiest is not null)
		{
			anchor = view.Peaks.FirstOrDefault(p => p.Frame == busiest.Key.AnchorFrame && p.Bin == busiest.Key.AnchorBin);
			SelectAnchorPairs();
		}

		await RedrawAsync();
	}

	private void SelectAnchorPairs() =>
		anchorPairs = anchor is { } a && view is not null
			? [.. view.Pairs.Where(p => p.AnchorFrame == a.Frame && p.AnchorBin == a.Bin).OrderBy(p => p.DeltaFrames)]
			: [];

	[JSInvokable]
	public async Task OnPeakPicked(int index)
	{
		if (view is null || index < 0 || index >= view.Peaks.Count)
		{
			return;
		}

		anchor = view.Peaks[index];
		SelectAnchorPairs();
		await RedrawAsync();
		StateHasChanged();
	}

	private async Task RedrawAsync()
	{
		if (view is null)
		{
			return;
		}

		var options = Catalogue.Options;
		var spec = view.Spectrogram;
		var stride = Math.Max(1, view.Pairs.Count / 90);
		var figure = new
		{
			frames = spec.Frames,
			bins = spec.Bins,
			frameSeconds = options.FrameDuration.TotalSeconds,
			hzPerBin = (double)options.SampleRate / options.WindowSize,
			maxHz = options.SampleRate / 2,
			startSeconds = view.StartSeconds,
			image = imageDirty ? spec.Image : null,
			peaks = view.Peaks.SelectMany(p => new[] { p.Frame, p.Bin }).ToArray(),
			pairs = view.Pairs.Where((_, i) => i % stride == 0).SelectMany(p => new[] { p.AnchorFrame, p.AnchorBin, p.PartnerFrame, p.PartnerBin }).ToArray(),
			zone = anchor is { } a ? new[] { a.Frame + 1, a.Bin - 128, a.Frame + options.MaxDeltaFrames, a.Bin + 127 } : null,
			selectedPairs = anchorPairs.SelectMany(p => new[] { p.AnchorFrame, p.AnchorBin, p.PartnerFrame, p.PartnerBin }).ToArray(),
			anchor = anchor is { } b ? new[] { b.Frame, b.Bin } : null,
			showSpectrogram,
			showPeaks,
			showPairs
		};
		imageDirty = false;
		await (await ModuleAsync()).InvokeVoidAsync("drawExplain", "explainCanvas", figure);
	}

	private Task RunClipAsync()
	{
		if (CurrentTrack is { } track)
		{
			trial = Lab.RunClip(track, clipStart, shift, ClipSeconds);
		}

		return Task.CompletedTask;
	}

	private async Task SweepAsync()
	{
		if (CurrentTrack is not { } track)
		{
			return;
		}

		sweeping = true;
		StateHasChanged();
		await Task.Yield();
		sweep = [.. Lab.SweepPhases(track, clipStart, ClipSeconds)];
		sweeping = false;
	}

	private double Misalignment(ClipTrial row)
	{
		var startSample = (clipStart * DemoCatalogue.SampleRate) + row.ShiftSamples;
		var fraction = (startSample % ExplainLab.Hop) / (double)ExplainLab.Hop;
		return Math.Min(fraction, 1 - fraction);
	}

	private string SweepShare(bool min)
	{
		var shares = sweep.Select(r => 100.0 * r.AtTrueOffset / Math.Max(1, r.ClipLandmarks));
		return (min ? shares.Min() : shares.Max()).ToString("0");
	}

	private double FrameSeconds(int frame) => (view?.StartSeconds ?? 0) + (frame * Catalogue.Options.FrameDuration.TotalSeconds);

	private string HzOf(int bin) => (bin * (double)Catalogue.Options.SampleRate / Catalogue.Options.WindowSize).ToString("0");

	private static string Bits(int value, int width) => Convert.ToString(value, 2).PadLeft(width, '0');

	private double OffsetSeconds(double frames) => frames * Catalogue.Options.FrameDuration.TotalSeconds;

	private string OffsetLabel(int bin) => OffsetSeconds((trial?.HistogramFirstFrame ?? 0) + (bin * (trial?.HistogramBinFrames ?? 1))).ToString("0.00") + " s";

	private bool IsPeakBin(int bin)
	{
		if (trial is null)
		{
			return false;
		}

		var from = trial.HistogramFirstFrame + (bin * trial.HistogramBinFrames);
		return trial.FoundOffsetFrames >= from && trial.FoundOffsetFrames < from + trial.HistogramBinFrames;
	}

	private double MarkerPercent()
	{
		if (trial is null || trial.Histogram.Count == 0)
		{
			return 0;
		}

		var total = trial.Histogram.Count * trial.HistogramBinFrames;
		return Math.Clamp((trial.TrueOffsetFrames - trial.HistogramFirstFrame) / total * 100, 0, 100);
	}

	public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
	{
		Catalogue.Changed -= OnCatalogueChanged;
		self?.Dispose();
		if (module is not null)
		{
			await module.DisposeAsync();
		}
	}
}
